xnu (12377.121.6+pie8) blueberry-pie-os; urgency=medium

  * A third kernel, rk3566, for the Rockchip RK3566 of the Anbernic
    RG353V: 16K pages, built for the Cortex-A55, with drivers for its
    GICv3, its DesignWare UART and watchdog and its SD card slots. pieboot
    picks it from the bundle by the board's compatible.
  * The Raspberry Pi's SDHCI driver and the RK3566's DesignWare MMC driver
    share the SD card protocol.
  * The kernel's console also goes to a RAM console that pieboot names,
    for the next system to read after a reset.
  * The watchdog driver also runs a DesignWare watchdog.
  * The xHCI driver leaves the RK3566's USB controllers alone. Their PHYs
    and clocks are not set up yet.
  * Making the first kext's text executable flushes the whole TLB. On the
    RK3566 its constructors took an instruction fetch fault on about one
    boot in eight.
  * kern.version names each kernel's build again, such as
    DEVELOPMENT_ARM64_BCM2712, so /etc/rc finds the SoC and loads the
    kexts.conf lines tagged for it.
  * The early kexts for the Raspberry Pi's devices load on its kernels
    only.
  * Kexts can drive USB devices through <pie/usb.h>. A bulk endpoint can
    keep its transfers queued, as a network adapter needs.
  * PIEUSBEthernet.kext drives USB Ethernet adapters with a Realtek
    RTL8152, RTL8153 or RTL8153B.
    It is not an early kext: list it in /etc/kexts.conf to load it.
  * The xHCI driver refuses a USB device whose device or configuration
    descriptor comes back short or with a bad header, and uses only the
    whole descriptors of its configuration.
  * The xHCI driver aligns its DMA buffers by bus address. A 64 KiB bulk
    transfer could cross a 64 KiB boundary, and a USB storage device could
    stop working after about 15 attaches.
  * The xHCI driver runs a hub with multiple TTs in its single-TT default
    setting, and says so to the controller.
  * Device-tree properties with names of 32 to 255 characters, which
    pieboot passes encoded, reach drivers under their full names.

 -- Maxwell <maxwell@blueberrypieos.com>  Wed, 18 Nov 2026 15:00:00 +0000

xnu (12377.121.6+pie6) blueberry-pie-os; urgency=medium

  * A pool on file vdevs opens: a file's attributes now include its type.
  * File vdevs are closed on unmount only while the system shuts down. A
    dataset unmount closed them under I/O, which gave read errors.
  * A zvol's I/O runs on the zvol taskqs and reports its errors. A pool on
    a zvol of another pool hung under writes and at import, and a failed
    zvol read or write reported success.
  * A remount does not make a dataset writable while its readonly property
    is on, so a read-only root stays read-only.
  * A remount that names another dataset than the one mounted is refused.
    It renamed the mount's source to that dataset.
  * A remount holds the file system's teardown lock as a reader, so a
    rollback or receive cannot replace the dataset under it.
  * A rollback or receive no longer purges the name cache while it holds
    the file system's teardown lock, where a vnode reclaim could deadlock.
  * A last zvol close that waited for the suspend lock keeps it, and an
    open or close that retries does not release a suspend lock it gave up.
  * blueberry-pie-os-kernel-dev installs the headers of /usr/local/include
    in /usr/include. A ZFS root keeps /usr/local on its data sets, where
    dpkg installs nothing.
  * The kernel maps the watchdog's registers at every boot. Its first try
    could come before their addresses were known. It then left running
    the watchdog that pieboot arms for a fallback boot, which reset the
    board about 16 s into the boot.
  * When the kernel cannot read the firmware's revision, it still sets or
    disarms a watchdog that pieboot armed. So does the watchdog driver. A
    failed map of the watchdog's registers is tried again.
  * A firmware call made before the mailbox is ready no longer leaks its
    message buffer.
  * The root pool imports from a card that held a larger pool before it
    was written again. The boot import read the whole disk too, found the
    old pool's labels at the card's end and took them for the new pool's.
  * On the Pi 4, whose kernel has smaller pages than user space, data a
    process passed to the kernel is freed again. Its last page stayed
    mapped in the kernel and kept the whole buffer, so every kext load
    leaked it, about 4 MB at each soft reboot.

 -- Maxwell <maxwell@blueberrypieos.com>  Mon, 16 Nov 2026 18:28:11 +0000

xnu (12377.121.6+pie4) blueberry-pie-os; urgency=medium

  * ZFS is built into the kernel: OpenZFS 2.4.1, which mounts a pool's
    boot dataset as the root with zfs_boot=POOL in the boot-args.
  * The ARC's maximum is a quarter of the memory and its minimum 64 MiB
    by default; zfs_arc_max= and zfs_arc_min= in the boot-args set other
    sizes, within the limits ZFS accepts.
  * Process 1 may switch the root with pivot_root(2) and unmount the old
    root, for a restart of user space on another root.
  * The kernel package provides pie-zfs-abi (= 2.4), the ZFS interface the
    zfs tools need.
  * A pool's boot dataset mounted as the root names the dataset as the
    mount's source.
  * The postinst updates the boot partition only when rpi-firmware's
    blueberry-pie-os-update-boot is installed.
  * ZFS encryption uses the PMULL instructions for GCM where the CPU has
    them, as the Pi 5's does.

 -- Maxwell <maxwell@blueberrypieos.com>  Sat, 14 Nov 2026 21:44:23 +0000

xnu (12377.121.6+pie3) blueberry-pie-os; urgency=medium

  * pie_watchdog_boot=N in the boot-args: the kernel panics unless user
    space checks in with the watchdog within N seconds of boot.
  * A watchdog the boot loader armed is set again while the kernel waits
    for the firmware.

